1
00:01:12,857 --> 00:01:18,857
World of Tomorrow is a animation, drama, science
fiction film directed by Don Hertzfeldt.

2
00:02:25,714 --> 00:02:31,714
A little girl is taken on a mind-bending tour of her distant future.

3
00:03:38,571 --> 00:03:44,571
Emily (voice), played by Julia Pott.

4
00:04:51,428 --> 00:04:57,428
Emily Prime (voice), played by Winona Mae.

5
00:06:04,285 --> 00:06:10,285
Simon (voice) (uncredited), played by Sara Cushman.

6
00:07:17,142 --> 00:07:23,142
World of Tomorrow is a series of animated science fiction short films 
written, directed, produced, animated, and edited by Don Hertzfeldt.

7
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:36,000
The series began with World of Tomorrow (2015), followed by World of Tomorrow Episode Two: The Burden of Other
People's Thoughts (2017) and World of Tomorrow Episode Three: The Absent Destinations of David Prime (2020).

8
00:09:42,857 --> 00:09:48,857
The first film was nominated for Best Animated
Short Film at the 2015 Academy Awards.

9
00:10:55,714 --> 00:11:01,714
All three films feature the voice of Julia Pott, frequently alongside Hertzfeldt's
four-year-old niece Winona Mae, who was recorded while drawing and playing.

10
00:12:08,571 --> 00:12:14,571
Her spontaneous, natural vocal reactions and questions were then
edited into the stories to create her character.

11
00:13:21,428 --> 00:13:27,428
Indiewire called the first film "one of the greatest short films in the history of movies," and Rolling Stone ranked World of Tomorrow #10 on its list of the "Greatest Animated
Movies Ever."
Of the "dreamy, beloved" ongoing series,The Film Stage noted, "Hertzfeldt has crafted what might be the crowning achievement of modern science fiction."

12
00:14:34,285 --> 00:14:40,285
The film explores themes of future, virtual reality,
immortality, narration, time travel.

13
00:15:47,142 --> 00:15:53,142
The tone is emotional and thought_provoking and mind_bending.
